WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.695 --> 00:00:05.540
GraFlag is a distributed benchmarking
platform for graph anomaly detection.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.326
It runs every method the same way,
on a GPU cluster,

3
00:00:09.326 --> 00:00:11.893
and evaluates every result the same way.

4
00:00:13.595 --> 00:00:17.333
A thin client drives
a Docker Swarm cluster over SSH.

5
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.988
Each method is a container image,
built from a pinned commit of its authors' code.

6
00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:25.119
Each experiment runs once,
as a Swarm service,

7
00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:29.460
and methods, datasets and results
live on shared NFS storage.

8
00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:34.410
From the command line,
graflag status shows the cluster:

9
00:00:34.426 --> 00:00:37.505
a manager and four GPU workers, all ready.

10
00:00:39.301 --> 00:00:43.658
It ships thirty-three detection methods,
from the seventeen PyGOD detectors

11
00:00:43.658 --> 00:00:48.480
on one shared image to dedicated integrations
of dynamic and streaming detectors,

12
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.011
and forty-three datasets, downloaded
from their original sources on first use.

13
00:00:56.538 --> 00:01:01.379
A method is a dot-env file,
a Dockerfile and a short integration script.

14
00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:08.262
The dot-env names the authors' repository
and pins the exact commit to build from,

15
00:01:08.262 --> 00:01:11.400
so a rebuild next year
still runs the same code.

16
00:01:12.252 --> 00:01:16.331
Contract tests check every method
definition before anything is built.

17
00:01:18.974 --> 00:01:21.450
One command runs a method on a dataset.

18
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.831
Swarm places the service
on a worker with a free GPU,

19
00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:27.588
and the method's output
streams back as it runs.

20
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:34.072
Meanwhile, GraFlag samples the memory
and GPU use of the whole process tree,

21
00:01:34.072 --> 00:01:36.722
so every method is measured the same way.

22
00:01:41.254 --> 00:01:44.730
When it finishes,
RARE reports its own AUROC,

23
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.742
and the runner adds the time
and memory it measured.

24
00:01:47.742 --> 00:01:49.916
Then the finished service is removed.

25
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:55.064
Evaluation is a second,
short-lived service.

26
00:01:55.064 --> 00:01:57.881
It computes the same metrics
for every method:

27
00:01:57.898 --> 00:02:02.655
AUC-ROC, average precision,
and precision and recall at k.

28
00:02:04.378 --> 00:02:08.235
Everything is also available
from the web dashboard: the cluster's health,

29
00:02:08.235 --> 00:02:12.731
the method and dataset catalogues,
and every experiment, updated live.

30
00:02:14.169 --> 00:02:17.181
Launching a run takes
two selections and a click.

31
00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:21.729
This one runs AnoGraph,
a sketch-based detector for streaming graphs,

32
00:02:21.729 --> 00:02:23.998
on the ISCX intrusion dataset.

33
00:02:30.562 --> 00:02:33.319
Once it completes, one click evaluates it.

34
00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:44.402
Its metrics appear beside the experiment,
with a thumbnail of every plot.

35
00:02:46.544 --> 00:02:48.449
Each plot opens full size,

36
00:02:48.449 --> 00:02:52.266
from the precision-recall curve
to the memory the run used over time.

37
00:02:58.262 --> 00:03:00.762
Every run saves its full configuration.

38
00:03:00.762 --> 00:03:03.667
Replaying it with from-config
runs the same method,

39
00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.257
with the same parameters,
on the same data.

40
00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:13.654
Swarm placed the replay on worker three;
the original ran on worker one.

41
00:03:19.163 --> 00:03:23.901
AnoGraph is deterministic,
so the replay produced exactly the same scores,

42
00:03:23.901 --> 00:03:28.849
and the same AUC-ROC
as the original: 0.948.

43
00:03:30.428 --> 00:03:32.464
GraFlag is open source.

44
00:03:32.464 --> 00:03:34.797
Install it with pip install graflag,

45
00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:38.042
and read the documentation
to benchmark your own methods.
